2,Bazigar language — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/bazigar_language,"External names include Bazigar, derived from the Persian term for performers, reflecting the community's historical occupation as acrobats and musicians. [4] Linguistically, Ethnologue classifies Bazigar within the Indo-European language family, specifically under the Indo-Iranian branch of Indo-Aryan languages. [5]",Bazigar,Indo-European
3,8. Bazigar article,https://punjab.global.ucsb.edu/sites/secure.lsit.ucsb.edu.gisp.d7_sp/files/sitefiles/journals/volume18/8_Bazigar_article.pdf,"In these respects, the present exposition of the Bazigar and their performing arts reveals aspects of Punjab’s music that one will rarely find discussed elsewhere, as well as the Bazigar involvement in practices that are near and dear to many.",Bazigar,Indo-European
4,Bazigar — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/Bazigar,"The Bazigar community has historically sustained itself through nomadic performances of acrobatic feats, traveling in family bands across rural Punjab and neighboring regions to entertain villagers at fairs, festivals, and weddings in exchange for cash, food, and clothing.",Bazigar,Indo-European
